---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const registry = `bo-shell        the app shell     → sidebar collapses to icon rail  (≤ 56rem)
bo-table        table container   → density compaction + col hiding  (≤ 30rem)
bo-widget-grid  dashboard grid    → span-2 widgets collapse          (≤ 41rem)
bo-widget       each widget       → components inside adapt to WIDGET width
bo-stepper      the stepper       → labels collapse to SR-only       (≤ 30rem)`;
---
<Gallery title="Container queries" description="Components respond to their container width, never the viewport, and every container query is named — the build fails on an anonymous one.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Components respond to their <em>container's</em> width, never the viewport — a
    table in a half-width dashboard widget compacts while the same table full-width
    doesn't. And a build rule with teeth: <strong>every <code>@container</code> query
    is named; the build fails on a bare one.</strong>
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The named-container registry</h2>
    <pre><code>{registry}</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why bare queries are banned</h2>
    <p>An element cannot query itself: a bare <code>@container</code> resolves against
    the <em>nearest ancestor</em> container — whatever that happens to be — or falls
    back to the small viewport when none exists. The same rule then means different
    things in different placements. This bug class recurred twice in review (a widget
    rule that keyed off the app shell; a stepper that collapsed on shell width);
    naming the container makes the dependency explicit and the mistake impossible,
    so the build enforces it mechanically.</p>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li>Thresholds are in <strong>rem</strong> (beyond-conformance support for users who raise the browser default font size).</li>
      <li>Explicit <code>data-density</code> beats auto-compaction when set
      on the table or its container itself — see the
      <a href={base + '/concepts/density'}>density rules</a> for the exact
      precedence.</li>
      <li>Compaction may compact — it may not make data unreachable: the
      <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data-table reflow
      requirement</a> (WCAG 1.4.10) is the canonical rule.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/concepts/density", "Density"], ["/components/data-table", "Data table"]]} />
</Gallery>
